DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ccppddiirr copies the directory hierarchy rooted at _d_i_r_1 onto
     _d_i_r_2, creating _d_i_r_2 (via mmkkddiirr) if it does not already ex-
     ist.  Regular files, subdirectories, and (for the superuser)
     device special files and named pipes are all copied; hierar-
     chies may straddle device boundaries.

     Files within _d_i_r_1 that have more than one link to each other
     are recreated as links to each other under _d_i_r_2 instead of
     as separate copies.  Links to files outside the _d_i_r_1 hierar-
     chy, or that cannot be recreated (for example, because the
     target crosses a device boundary), are reported at the end
     of the run as missed or broken external links, provided -vv
     or -tt was given.

     If a target file corresponding to a source file already ex-
     ists and is not a directory, ccppddiirr unlinks it before
     copying.  If _d_i_r_1 and _d_i_r_2 turn out to be the same directory
     (or one nested inside the copy of the other), that directory
     is skipped to avoid an infinite copy.

     Copied files get the source file's permission bits.  When
     run by the superuser, the owner, group, and full mode
     (including the sticky bit) are preserved as well; otherwise
     the invoking user owns the new files and the sticky bit is
     not copied.

     -dd   Give each copied file the source file's last-modified
